  "handoff": {
    "summary": "Implemented and audited the source-portable dense binary64 linear algebra slice, then added domain-owned detached capability discovery and no-computation planning for every operation. Results now provide bounded structured explanations, accessible static PlotSpec views for factorization, conditioning, convergence, and validation, plus bounded PlotAnimation traces for pedagogically useful factorization and iterative progress. Executable examples cover success, Hilbert conditioning, and structured failure in CPython and Sage.js.",
    "risks": [
      "Only Linux x64 CPython and Sage.js have a recorded runtime receipt; browser, Linux arm64, macOS arm64, and Windows x64 remain explicit target work.",
      "The lane intentionally leaves shared exports, diagnostic/status registries other than its consumption of maximum_elapsed_time, package graphs, Pyright registration, and test-script discovery to integration.",
      "This is an O(m*n*min(m,n)) ordinary-Python reference implementation, not an accelerated large-problem backend.",
      "Animations require an iterations-or-more-detailed trace; retained semantic frames remain subject to the caller's trace event and byte budgets."
    ],
    "next_steps": [
      "Integration should apply docs/numerical-computing/linear-algebra/integration-requests.md, including the central lazy facade over the package-local capabilities, supports, and plan functions.",
      "Qualify the identical corpus on browser and the three pending native host classes before adding those platforms to validated_platforms.",
      "Use the reference slice as the differential fallback for later accelerated, sparse, and spectral work."
    ]
  }
